[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Client-Side Prediction, Server Reconciliation, and Lag Compensation in Unreal Engine"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Developing responsive multiplayer games requires overcoming network latency. Without mitigation, players experience frustrating input delays. Unreal Engine's Character Movement Component provides built-in solutions for Client-Side Prediction, Server Reconciliation, and Lag Compensation to ensure fluid and fair gameplay."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Core Concepts"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Client-Side Prediction: The local client immediately simulates character movement locally before receiving confirmation from the server, eliminating input lag."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Server Reconciliation: The server periodically sends authoritative state updates. If the client prediction deviates from the server state beyond a threshold, the client corrects its position."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Lag Compensation: A server-side technique where the server rewinds world history to accurately register hitscan weapon fire based on the shooter's historical perspective."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Leveraging the Character Movement Component"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unreal Engine handles most client-side prediction and server reconciliation automatically via ACharacter and UCharacterMovementComponent. When designing custom movement modes, you must ensure RPCs and network smoothing function correctly."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"void AMyCustomCharacter::ServerMove_Implementation(FVector_NetQuantize101 InVelocity, FVector_NetQuantize100 InLocation)
{
    // Server validates and applies the authoritative movement
    Super::ServerMove_Implementation(InVelocity, InLocation);
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing Lag Compensation for Projectiles and Hits"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"To handle high-latency scenarios during combat, you must query historical transforms of other players using capsules or skeletal meshes. Unreal Engine provides built-in rewind features via `FScopedMovementEnabler` or custom position history structures."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"void AMyGameMode::ProcessHitscan(AMyCharacter* Shooter, FVector TraceStart, FVector TraceEnd, float HitTime)
{
    // Rewind game state to HitTime
    RewindWorldToTime(HitTime);

    // Perform line trace against historical positions
    FHitResult HitResult;
    GetWorld()->LineTraceSingleByChannel(HitResult, TraceStart, TraceEnd, ECC_Pawn);

    // Restore current world state
    RestoreWorldState();

    if (HitResult.bBlockingHit)
    {
        ApplyDamage(HitResult.GetActor(), Shooter);
    }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Network Optimization"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Validate all critical client inputs on the server to prevent cheating."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Limit the maximum historical rewind time (e.g., maximum 300ms) to prevent abuse and excessive memory overhead."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Utilize `FVector_NetQuantize` and `FVector_NetQuantize100` to compress network bandwidth for movement replication."}]
